"Landmarks in Mechanical Engineering Published | Newswise (https://www.newswise.com/articles/landmarks-in-mechanical-engineering-published)\nciteturn124search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 29.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\n# Landmarks in Mechanical Engineering Published\n\n17-Jan-1997 at 12:00 AM EST, by ASME (American Society of Mechanical Engineers)\n\nFor Immediate Release\nContact: John Varrasi\n212-705-8158 [email protected]\n\nLANDMARKS IN MECHANICAL ENGINEERING PUBLISHED\n\nNew York, Jan. 9, 1996 -- Landmarks in Mechanical Engineering compiles 135 inventions and other technological achievements set apart for their contribution to scientific growth, industrial development and human progress.\n\nThe new book is announced jointly by ASME International (American Society of Mechanical Engineers) and Purdue University Press, the publisher.\n\nLargely a chronicle of the 25-year-old ASME History and Heritage program, Landmarks in Mechanical Engineering includes what engineers, researchers and historians consider to be the most significant and the most remarkable in the world, from the era of the Industrial Revolution in England to the Space Age in the United States.\n\nThe 135 engineering landmarks in the volume are grouped into main categories including Rail Transportation, Electrical Power Production, Minerals Extraction and Refining, Manufacturing Facilities and Processes, Communications and Data Processing, Pumping and 10 other headings.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLandmarks in mechanical engineering / ASME International History and Heritage | Smithsonian Institution (https://www.si.edu/object/landmarks-mechanical-engineering-asme-international-history-and-heritage%3Asiris_sil_512709)\nciteturn124search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; \n\n# Landmarks in mechanical engineering / ASME International History and Heritage\n\nSmithsonian Libraries and Archives\n\n## Object Details\n\n### Author\n\nAmerican Society of Mechanical Engineers History and Heritage Committee\n\n### Date\n\n1997\n\nC1997\n\n### Type\n\nBooks\n\n### Place\n\nUnited States\n\n### Topic\n\nMechanical engineering--History\n\n### Physical description\n\nxvii, 364 p. : ill. ; 26 cm\n\n### Metadata Usage\n\nCC0\n\n### Record ID\n\nsiris_sil_512709\n\narrow-up Back to top--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLandmarks in Mechanical Engineering - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books?id=00FSAAAAMAAJ)\nciteturn124search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 29.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\n# Landmarks in Mechanical Engineering\n\nImage: Front CoverPurdue University Press, 1997 - Technology & Engineering - 364 pages The book describes, and where possible illustrates, historic mechanical engineering landmarks, representing the accomplishments of mechanical engineers over the past 250 years-from the steam engine of Thomas Newcomen (1712), which launched the Industrial Revolution, to the Saturn V rocket (1967). Some of them are recognizable to us all, such as Xerography, Sikorsky's helicopter, and Disneyland's monorail. Others work behind the scenes or have been superseded, such as the Owens AR bottle machine and the Jackson Ferry Shot Tower. The landmarks are grouped into such categories as mechanical power production, power transmission, minerals extraction and refining, food processing, environmental control, transportation, biomedical engineering, and communications and data processing. Each section is introduced by an essay that helps place each landmark into the context of technical and general history. While some of these landmarks have not survived intact, others can be visited, and travel information is provided. The machines that shape our society help educate us about an important facet of human history and how technology has influenced it. ASME International's landmarks program identifies and recognizes artifacts of significance before they disappear. This roster of landmarks tells a magnificent story of people and places and of innovation and discovery.\n\n### From inside the book\n\n[Input][Input][Input] [Input]\n\n### Contents\n\nPumping  | 1\n | \nMechanical Power Production  | 24\n | \n\nElectrical Power Production  | 57\n | \nCopyright  | \n | \n\n16 other sections not shown\n\n### Other editions - View all\n\n | Landmarks in Mechanical Engineering\nNo preview available - 1997\n | \n\n### Common terms and phrases\n\naircraft American Society atomic beam boiler brake building built Canal cars Center century coal Company construction cooling Corliss crane crawler cylinder diameter diesel district heating driving Duquesne Incline EBR-I Edison efficiency electric energy feet forging fuel furnace FURTHER READING gas turbine gear heat History horsepower hour hydraulic inches industry installed invention iron Kaplan turbine km/hr laboratory landmarks largest launch Liberty ship located Location/Access Lowell machine machinery manufacturing Mechanical Engineers miles mill Morris Canal motor Museum nuclear Ohio operation patent Pennsylvania pipe piston power plant powerhouse Press pressure produced propeller psig pumping engine Railroad Railway reactor reciprocating engines River rocket rotating screw shaft ship speed Springfield Armory Station steam engine steam locomotives steam turbine steel Street supplied temperature Today tunnel Turbinia United valve vertical vessel Washington water-tube boiler waterwheel welding wheel York\n\n### Bibliographic information\n\nTitle  | Landmarks in Mechanical Engineering\nHistory of Technology Series\nContributor  | American Society of Mechanical Engineers. History and Heritage Committee\nEdition  | illustrated\nPublisher  | Purdue University Press, 1997\nOriginal from  | the University of Michigan\nDigitized  | Dec 5, 2007\nISBN  | 1557530947, 9781557530943\nLength  | 364 pages\nSubjects  | Travel › United States › General\nTechnology & Engineering / Engineeri