\n621'.0973—dc20 96-31573  \nCIP--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAmerican Society of Mechanical Engineers Collection (https://sirismm.si.edu/EADpdfs/NMAH.AC.1522.pdf)\nciteturn124search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; \nSeries 2: National Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark\nBrochures\nAmerican Society of Mechanical Engineers Collection\nNMAH.AC.1522\nBox 4, Folder 6\nNational Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark Brochures, 1994\nBox 4, Folder 7\nNational Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark Brochures, 1995\nBox 4, Folder 8\nNational Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark Brochures, 1996\nBox 4, Folder 9\nNational Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark Brochures, 1997\nBox 4, Folder 10\nNational Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark Brochures, 1998\nBox 4, Folder 11\nNational Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark Brochures, nomination, 1998\nBox 4, Folder 12\nNational Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark Brochures, 2000\nBox 4, Folder 13\nNational Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark Brochures, 2001\nBox 4, Folder 14\nNational Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark Brochures, 2003\nBox 4, Folder 15\nHistoric Engineering Landmarks, brochure about program , 1983\nBox 4, Folder 16\nState of Florida Civil Engineering Landmarks, 1976\nReturn to Table of Contents\nPage 7 of 9--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEngineering Landmarks - ASME (https://www.asme.org/about-asme/engineering-history/landmarks)\nciteturn124search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; \n\n# Engineering Landmarks\n\nView Landmark by: \nLocation Designator\n\nHistoric Mechanical Engineering Landmarks are existing artifacts or systems representing a significant mechanical engineering technology. ASME has designated over 280 landmarks through its History and Heritage Landmarks Program since the program began in 1971. Explore our landmarks by designator, topic or world location.\nLandmarks by Topic\n\n##### [Button: Agriculture]\n\n#158 Fresno Scraper (1883) #190 Hart Parr Tractor (1903) #201 Cooper Steam Traction Engine Collection (1860–1883) #225 Rumely Companies' Agricultural Products (1853)\n\n##### [Button: Air and Space ]\n\n#36 RL-10 Rocket Engine (1958) #52 Saturn V Rocket (1967) #53 Saturn V Rocket (1967) #54 Saturn V Rocket (1967) #95 Sikorsky VS-300 Helicopter (1939) #102 Atlas Launch Vehicle (1957) #140 Arnold AFB Wind Tunnel (1955) 【13†#149 Hydromatic Propeller (ca. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHome - ASME papers & publications - LibGuides at MIT Libraries (https://libguides.mit.edu/asme-pubs)\nciteturn124search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; \n\n# ASME papers & publications: Home\n\n## Finding ASME papers & publications\n\nFinding American Society of Mechanical Engineers (ASME) papers can be tricky.   Here are some tips on searching for ASME papers, based on the date of publication.  For help accessing these papers see the box below.\n\n  * 1880 - 1988: Use finding aids (see below) to determine the paper number.  You may also search Compendex to try to determine the paper number or other useful information. See below for how to access these items in the MIT Libraries.\n  * 1989 - 2001: Search Compendex to try to determine the paper number or other useful information. See below for how to access these items in the MIT Libraries.\n  * 2002 - present: ASME Technical Papers are available online in the ASME Digital Collection.\n\nASME standards are not available online.  There are some ASME Standards in print in Barker Library (Click \"Find it in the library/Request item\" to see a list of standards). Here is the record in our collection.  These items are stored in the Library Annex. You may request the entire volume or a PDF of any individual paper(s) you need by clicking on the \"Find it in the Library/Request Item\" link.  \n  * GT or Gas Turbine papers: These items are stored in the Library Annex. You may request the entire volume or a PDF of any individual paper(s) you need by clicking on the \"Find it in the Library/Request Item\" link.  \n    * 1968 - 1981: See Item Record.\n    * 1982 - 1987: See Item Record.\n    * 1988 - 2002: See Item Record.\n  * WA or Winter Annual papers: These items are stored in the Library Annex. You may request the entire volume or a PDF of any individual paper(s) you need by clicking on the \"Find it in the Library/Request Item\" link.  \n    * 1960 - 1970: See Item Record.\n    * 1970 - 1998: See Item Record. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nASME Digital Collection - with ASME Standards | McMaster University Libraries (https://library.mcmaster.ca/databases/asme-digital-collection-american-society-mechanical-engineers)\nciteturn124search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; \n\n# ASME Digital Collection - with ASME Standards\n\nASME Digital Collection - with ASME Standards Connect\n\nAlternative Title: American Society of Mechanical Engineers Complete Digital Collection\n\nAllowable Uses:\n\nAccess is available on and off campus to current McMaster University students, faculty and staff.\n\nNotes:\n\nTo access the ASME Standards Collection select \"Standards\" and then \"click here to access via institutional account\" in upper right of screen.\n\nDescription:\n\nThe ASME Digital Collection is ASME’s authoritative, online reference for the mechanical engineering and related research communities. Includes topical collections on Automotive Systems, Biomedical Engineering, Nuclear Engineering, Renewable Energy and more.\n\n  * ASME’s Journals from 1960 – present\n  * ASME’s Conference Proceedings from 2000 – present, plus select proceedings back to 1955\n  * ASME eBooks selected from 1993 – present\n  * ASME Standards.  Select \"Standards\" and then \"click here to access via institutional account\" in upper right of screen.\n\nUser Type:\nMcMaster - MacID required\n\nDatabase Type:\nArticles\neBooks\nPatents & Standards\n\nSubjects:\nBiomedical Engineering\nEngineering\nMaterials Science and Engineering\nMechanical Engineering--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nL\nre Automatic Shrimp Peeling Machine, \nModel 'A', (https://qa12.asme.org/wwwasmeorg/media/resourcefiles/aboutasme/who%20we%20are/engineering%20history/landmarks/230-lapeyre-automatic-shrimp-peeling-machine.pdf)\nciteturn124search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.2 years ago; \nAffairs and Board on Public Information. For \nfurther information, please contact Public \nInformation at ASME, Three Park Avenue, New \nYork, NY 10016-5990, 1-212-591-7740.\nDesignation\nHarry Armen, P.E., President\nDouglas R. Brown, Vice President, Region X\nMark W. Powell, P.E., History and Heritage Chair, \n Region X\nVictoria Rockwell, Senior Vice President, \n Public Affairs\nMarc W. Goldsmith, P.E., Vice President, \n Public Information\nVirgil R. Carter, FAIA, Executive Director\nASME History and Heritage Committee\nSince the History and Heritage Program began \nin 1971, 229 landmarks have been designated \nas historic mechanical engineering landmarks, \nheritage collections or heritage sites. Each \nrepresents a progressive step in the evolution of \nmechanical engineering and its significance to \nsociety in general. Site designations note an event \nor development of clear historic importance \nto mechanical engineers. Collections mark the \ncontributions of a number of objects with special \nsignificance to the historical development of \nmechanical engineering.\nR. Michael Hunt, P.E., History and Heritage Chair\nRobert M. Vogel, Secretary\nJohn K. Brown\nRobert Friedel\nRichard I. Pawliger, P.E.\nPaul J. Torpey, Past President\nHerman Viegas, P.E.\nDiane Kaylor, Staff Liaison\nWil Haywood, Public Information Coordinator\n The Landmarks Program illuminates our \ntechnological heritage and encourages the \npreservation of the physical remains of historically \nimportant works. It provides an annotated roster \nfor engineers, students, educators, historians and \ntravelers. It helps establish persistent reminders \nof where we have been and where we are going \nalong the divergent paths of discovery.  \nASME New Orleans Section \n \nHarold W. Baur, P.E., Chair\nJohn R. Muller, Vice-Chair\nDenise V. Boihem, Treasurer\nLeif C. Wismar, Secretary\nMichael J. Gumina, P.E., History and Heritage Chair\n23"
